      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Postoperative analgesia in children may be improved by using tramadol. The pharmacokinetics of rectal tramadol in young children were therefore investigated."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The pharmacokinetics of rectal tramadol and its active metabolite were studied in 12 young children (age: 1-6 yr) postoperatively. On the basis of these data, a population model was constructed. Using this model, the pharmacokinetics of different doses of tramadol were calculated."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The pharmacokinetics of rectal tramadol could be adequately described by a one-compartment model. The pharmacokinetic parameters derived from the model indicate that a low variability was present. Elimination half-life was 4.3 (0.2) h (sem) and the apparent clearance was 16.4 (1.5) litre h(-1) (sem)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The study showed that after rectal administration, tramadol is absorbed at a reasonable rate and with a low inter-individual variability in small children. The data also suggested that a rectal dose of tramadol 1.5-2.0 mg kg(-1) is therapeutic."
        }
      ]},
